            <ArticleTitle>Cessation of ureteral colic does not necessarily mean that a ureteral stone has been expelled.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-5347(17)77794-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.juro.2017.10.032</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate whether cessation of renal colic is consistent with an expelled ureteral stone or whether imaging may be indicated even in the absence of symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective study was performed of patients who presented to our institution with acute renal colic and ureteral stone and subsequently were evaluated at a follow-up visit where they reported complete cessation of pain for at least 72 hours.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Fifty-two (52) patients met inclusion criteria and reported no pain for at least 72 hours at the time of follow up visit. Fourteen patients (14/52 patients, 26% of total cohort) demonstrated a persistent ureteral stone despite denying any associated symptoms. Multivariate logistic regression did not show an association between stone size or stone location and the likelihood of passage in this cohort.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Cessation of pain was associated with passage of ureteral stone in nearly 75% of this study cohort, but 26% of patients still had persistent ureteral stones. We recommend routine follow up imaging in all patients with ureteral stones to document stone passage and avoid the risks of silent ureteral obstruction.</AbstractText>
